<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.12.4: Recognizing Points Along Boundary Line</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00591</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.12.4: Recognizing Points Along Boundary Line</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6639380a-2450-48ab-9621-b84ed4b8101e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>This activity is an extension of the expectations in the TEKS.</para>
      <para>For questions 1 – 6, use the following scenario:</para>
      <para>
        <quote>A popular band is trying to raise at least $20,000 for charity by holding multiple concerts at a park. It plans to sell tickets at $25 each. For each 2-hour concert, the band would need to pay the park $1,250 in fees for security, cleaning, and traffic services.</quote>
        <quote>The band needs to find the combinations of number of tickets sold, \( t \), and number of concerts held, \( c \), that would allow it to reach its fundraising goal.</quote>
     </para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write an inequality to represent the constraints in this situation.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\( 25t - 1,250c \geq 20,000 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--START QUESTION #2 -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item> Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph your inequality from question 1 that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool below.</item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><!--BEGIN ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
            <para>When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <!--REPLACE IMAGE BELOW WITH CORRECT IMAGE-->
            <figure>
              <media alt="Graph of an inequality. Number of concerts held. Number of tickets sold.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_14_09_01.svg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--END ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Name two possible combinations of number of tickets sold and number of concerts held that would allow the band to meet its goal.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <list>
            <item>\( (1000, 2) \)</item>
            <item>\( (1400, 5) \)</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>For questions 4 – 6, decide which combination of tickets and concerts would mean more money for charity:</para>
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>1,300 tickets and 10 concerts, or 1,300 tickets and 5 concerts?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>1,300 tickets and 5 concerts</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>1,600 tickets and 16 concerts, or 1,200 tickets and 9 concerts?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>1,600 tickets and 16 concerts.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>2,000 tickets and 4 concerts, or 2,500 tickets and 10 concerts?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>2,500 tickets and 10 concerts</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
